WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.280 --> 00:00:03.100
It’s hard to find something everybody agrees
on.

00:00:03.100 --> 00:00:04.100
Crunchy or smooth?

00:00:04.160 --> 00:00:04.800
Smooth.

00:00:04.920 --> 00:00:06.300
Mac or PC?

00:00:06.360 --> 00:00:07.680
Fries or onion rings?

00:00:07.780 --> 00:00:12.440
Fries. But if there’s one financial instrument
that seems universally beloved, it’s got

00:00:12.450 --> 00:00:15.599
to be the 401(k).

00:00:15.599 --> 00:00:16.599
Everybody loves ‘em!

00:00:16.599 --> 00:00:21.410
People delay saving for a home, building an
emergency fund, or even paying off high-interest

00:00:21.410 --> 00:00:27.620
debt in pursuit of this conquering hero: the
tall, dark, and handsome 401(k).

00:00:27.660 --> 00:00:28.960
My hero…

00:00:28.960 --> 00:00:31.340
There’s a whole lot to love with the 401(k).

00:00:31.340 --> 00:00:36.390
So saddle up and take a ride to find out what
makes this cowboy the darling of investors

00:00:36.390 --> 00:00:38.000
everywhere!

00:00:45.820 --> 00:00:51.699
The year was 1980, and The Revenue Act of
1978 was finally going into effect.

00:00:51.699 --> 00:00:57.829
And deep in the bill was a tiny provision;
Section 401, Subsection K. Largely overlooked,

00:00:57.829 --> 00:01:04.150
Section 401(k) allowed employees to defer
taxes on bonuses and stock options -- basically

00:01:04.150 --> 00:01:07.840
a way for rich executives to make more and
pay less tax on it.

00:01:07.840 --> 00:01:13.270
But everything changed in 1981 when the IRS
ruled that employees could also contribute

00:01:13.270 --> 00:01:14.940
from their SALARY.

00:01:14.940 --> 00:01:18.180
This was music to employer’s ears!

00:01:18.180 --> 00:01:23.240
Managing all those employee pensions was expensive,
complicated, and downright risky!

00:01:23.240 --> 00:01:28.299
Maybe this new fella, 401(k), could replace
the dusty old pensions of our grandpappies.

00:01:28.299 --> 00:01:32.260
Before long, 401(k) fever was spreading like
wildfire!

00:01:32.260 --> 00:01:37.560
By 1996, 401(k) accounts held over a trillion
dollars!

00:01:39.670 --> 00:01:40.680
How does it work?

00:01:40.689 --> 00:01:45.100
Fundamentally, a 401(k) is an employer-sponsored
investment account.

00:01:45.100 --> 00:01:50.310
It lets you invest part of your paycheck and
receive a tax benefit for doing so.

00:01:50.310 --> 00:01:55.340
Like company-provided insurance programs,
you have to opt-in to participate.

00:01:55.340 --> 00:01:58.310
The most attractive feature is the “employer
match”.

00:01:58.310 --> 00:02:03.240
Translation: if you save for your future,
the boss rewards you with “free” money,

00:02:03.240 --> 00:02:06.510
matching your contribution dollar for dollar,
up to a limit.

00:02:06.510 --> 00:02:11.090
Around half of 401(k)s offer an employer
match and this “free money” can come to

00:02:11.090 --> 00:02:12.870
thousands of extra dollars!

00:02:12.870 --> 00:02:15.200
Then there’s the sweet tax breaks.

00:02:15.200 --> 00:02:20.260
Depending on the type of 401(k), your contributions
could be “pre-tax”, meaning it lowers

00:02:20.260 --> 00:02:22.210
your taxable income for the year.

00:02:22.210 --> 00:02:26.220
OR, you can pay the tax now, and allow that
money to grow tax-free.

00:02:26.220 --> 00:02:29.460
The more you save, the less taxes you pay.

00:02:29.460 --> 00:02:32.069
Looking pretty spiffy over there, Mr. 401(k)!

00:02:32.069 --> 00:02:37.180
Oh -- and don’t forget that your contributions
are being invested in stock and bond funds.

00:02:37.180 --> 00:02:40.590
We have Two Cents episodes about those if
you’re not sure how they work.

00:02:40.590 --> 00:02:44.980
Now you can just sit back and watch compound
interest fatten your herd!

00:02:44.980 --> 00:02:47.790
Free money, big tax breaks, and investment
growth!

00:02:47.790 --> 00:02:49.880
What’s not to love?!

00:02:52.560 --> 00:02:54.400
Hold your horses there, partner.

00:02:54.400 --> 00:02:59.510
While the 401(k) has a lot going for it, there
are a couple burrs under the saddle.

00:02:59.510 --> 00:03:04.269
Like the risks of managing your own investment
portfolios instead of leaving it to the professionals.

00:03:04.269 --> 00:03:09.900
As 401(k)s soared in popularity during the
80’s and 90’s, billions of dollars flowed

00:03:09.900 --> 00:03:11.830
into risky sectors like tech-stocks.

00:03:11.830 --> 00:03:16.980
And when things came crashing down -- like
they did twice in the 2000’s -- many working

00:03:16.980 --> 00:03:20.599
folks were left adrift like a tumbleweed in
the wind.

00:03:20.599 --> 00:03:24.500
And did you know that “Mr 401(k)” doesn’t
work for free?

00:03:24.500 --> 00:03:30.360
A study by TD Ameritrade found that 73% of
participants didn’t know how much their

00:03:30.360 --> 00:03:36.860
401(k) costs, while 37% weren’t aware they
were paying fees at all!

00:03:36.860 --> 00:03:41.250
Investment firms regularly rack up hefty fees
since nobody’s paying attention -- with

00:03:41.250 --> 00:03:44.409
the average being around 1.4%!

00:03:44.409 --> 00:03:48.140
And remember that juicy employer “match”
we mentioned earlier?

00:03:48.140 --> 00:03:54.240
Well, that might not end up being yours thanks
to “401(k) vesting”.

00:03:54.240 --> 00:03:58.480
Even though funds might appear in your account,
they’re only yours once you become “vested”

00:03:58.480 --> 00:04:02.400
-- often 3 to 5 years after you get hired!

00:04:02.400 --> 00:04:07.079
With the vast majority of millennials only
expecting to stay in a job for a few years

00:04:07.080 --> 00:04:11.460
at the most, that’s a lot of “free money”
that never gets collected.

00:04:13.840 --> 00:04:19.650
Despite their perks, and general popularity,
401(k)s have left a societal legacy that’s,

00:04:19.650 --> 00:04:21.120
well, ugly.

00:04:21.120 --> 00:04:26.220
See, 401(k)s were originally designed to be
a supplement to worker pensions.

00:04:26.220 --> 00:04:31.120
For most of the 20th century, it was common
for workers to stay with a single employer

00:04:31.120 --> 00:04:32.660
for most of their lives.

00:04:32.660 --> 00:04:38.700
And for that loyalty, their company offered
a defined benefit pension for the worker’s golden years.

00:04:38.700 --> 00:04:43.540
These plans offered steadiness and security,
with the employer watching over everyone’s

00:04:43.550 --> 00:04:46.380
plans -- from the janitor to the CEO.

00:04:46.380 --> 00:04:51.810
By their peak in 1980, 38% of all private
sector workers had an employer pension.

00:04:51.810 --> 00:04:56.580
Today, thanks to the 401(k), only 13% of private
sector workers have a pension.

00:04:56.580 --> 00:05:01.570
And while 401(k)s have their perks, they're just not as stable or reliable.

00:05:01.570 --> 00:05:06.240
401(k)s are also “opt-in,” which means
you aren’t automatically enrolled.

00:05:06.240 --> 00:05:10.854
Left to their own devices, many employees simply aren’t saving enough -- if they’re

00:05:10.854 --> 00:05:11.900
saving at all.

00:05:11.900 --> 00:05:19.960
Of the 79% of workers eligible to save into
a 401(k), only 41% opt to participate.

00:05:19.960 --> 00:05:25.340
The National Institute on Retirement Security
finds that the median retirement savings balance

00:05:25.340 --> 00:05:32.850
is just $3,000 for all working-age households
and a mere $12,000 for those near-retirement!

00:05:32.850 --> 00:05:39.060
Most financial experts recommend a personal
retirement savings rate between 10 - 15%.

00:05:39.060 --> 00:05:42.220
The real rates are between 1 and 3%.

00:05:42.229 --> 00:05:46.970
The 401(k) was designed to be the side-dish,
not the main course.

00:05:46.970 --> 00:05:52.169
Saddling workers with the “opportunity”
to manage their own retirements has created

00:05:52.169 --> 00:05:54.870
a national crisis in retirement preparedness.

00:05:54.870 --> 00:05:59.789
But the good news is that if you know its
place, and use it wisely, a 401(k) can be

00:05:59.789 --> 00:06:02.289
a great part of your financial toolkit.

00:06:02.289 --> 00:06:06.530
Since pensions are going the way of the horse
and buggy, you’ll need your 401(k) to be

00:06:06.530 --> 00:06:11.020
galloping double-time to keep from being left
in the dust.

00:06:11.020 --> 00:06:15.300
Take advantage of it and you’ll be riding
into the sunset instead of off a cliff.

00:06:15.300 --> 00:06:17.500
And that’s our two cents!

00:06:19.160 --> 00:06:22.980
If you've been transitioned from a pension to a 401(k)

00:06:22.980 --> 00:06:24.980
tell us about experience in the comments!

